# expect: `ctl` introduces `ctl effect Name { ... }`
# `ctl pub effect` is the other order, and it is not the one. `pub` composes
# with a contextual modifier by coming first (`pub opaque type`), and effects
# follow that rather than inventing a second rule. The word after `ctl` is then
# `pub` rather than `effect`, which is the same one-token test the `ctl fn`
# case fails, so the reader is told what `ctl` introduces rather than what may
# start a declaration.
ctl pub effect Async {
  fn wait(ms: Int) -> Unit
}
